Chapter 3
The term structure, inflation, and what a bond is
هيكل أسعار الفائدة والتضخم وماهية السند
A company that wants money has to pay the going rate for it, and there is no single going rate. There is a rate for three months and a different one for ten years, a rate for a government and a higher one for a food manufacturer, a rate quoted today and an expectation of what it will be in a year. The pattern of those rates across time is the term structure, and a treasurer who can read it borrows better than one who cannot.
Behind the whole pattern sits inflation. A lender who expects prices to rise 3 per cent will not lend at 2, so expected inflation is built into every quoted rate, and a change in what people expect moves the price of every instrument that pays a fixed amount. This chapter sets out the structure of rates and the inflation inside them, and then describes the instrument the rest of the section is about: the bond.
Figure F603-01 is your map.
Figure F603-01. Chapter 3 roadmap: the shape of rates, the inflation inside them, and the anatomy of a bond.
