WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 built the required-return ladder, with a risk-free rate at the bottom and a premium for every kind of risk above it. Part 1 recorded Orontes's own borrowing: $6,000,000 of long-term debt, $400,000 of it due within the year, and $390,000 of interest paid. This chapter explains why the rate on that debt is what it is, and what would change it.
3.1  The term structure of interest rates
Plot the yield on government securities against their maturity and the result is the yield curve. It is normally upward-sloping: a ten-year security yields more than a one-year one. It can be flat, and occasionally it inverts, with short rates above long ones.
Three theories explain the shape, and the exam names all three.
The expectations theory says long rates are averages of expected future short rates. If one-year money costs 4 per cent now and is expected to cost 6 per cent next year, then two-year money must cost about the same as rolling over: 1.04 × 1.06 = 1.1024, which is about 4.99 per cent a year. On this theory an upward-sloping curve means the market expects rates to rise, and an inverted curve means it expects them to fall — which is why an inversion is watched as a recession signal, since rates fall when the economy weakens.
The liquidity preference theory adds that lenders dislike tying money up and require a premium for length, so the curve slopes upward even when no change in rates is expected. This explains why upward slopes are the normal case rather than the occasional one.
The market segmentation theory says that borrowers and lenders have preferred maturities — a pension fund wants thirty-year assets, a company financing stock wants ninety-day money — and that rates in each segment are set by supply and demand there. On this theory the curve can take any shape at all, and a large government issue at one maturity can move that part of the curve without telling you anything about expectations.
Figure F603-02 reads the three shapes.
Figure F603-02. Three yield curves and what each is taken to mean
@table
Shape | Short rates | Long rates | Usual reading
Upward-sloping | Lower | Higher | Normal: a term premium, or rates expected to rise
Flat | Equal | Equal | Rates expected to hold; a turning point
Inverted | Higher | Lower | Rates expected to fall; often a recession signal
@end
The curve moves for the reasons the outcome asks about. A central bank raising its policy rate lifts the short end directly. A rise in expected inflation lifts the whole curve, and the long end most. A flight to safety in a crisis pushes money into government securities and pushes long yields down. And a government borrowing heavily at one maturity lifts that segment.
EXAM TRAP   Yield curve questions are about direction
The exam rarely asks you to draw a curve. It asks what a stated change does to it, or what a stated shape implies.
A curve is drawn for one credit quality, normally government. A corporate curve sits above it by the credit spread, and the spread itself widens in a downturn.
An inverted curve is a signal, not a mechanism. It predicts nothing on its own.
