3.2  Inflation, interest rates and the price of an instrument
A lender gives up money now and gets money back later. If prices rise in between, the money that comes back buys less, so the lender requires compensation for the loss before any compensation for risk. That is why every nominal interest rate contains an inflation expectation.
The relationship is the Fisher effect, and it is multiplicative rather than additive. A lender wanting a real return of 2 per cent who expects inflation of 3 per cent requires 1.02 × 1.03 = 1.0506, that is 5.06 per cent. The approximation everybody uses, 2.0 + 3.0 = 5.0 per cent, is close at low rates and wrong by a full point at the rates of a high-inflation economy.
Two consequences matter for the exam.
Only expected inflation is in the rate. Inflation that was not expected transfers wealth: between a borrower and a lender at a fixed rate, unexpected inflation makes the borrower better off and the lender worse off, because the fixed payments are made in cheaper money. That is the point Book 1, Chapter 18 made about historical-cost debt ratios, reached from the other side.
A change in the expectation moves prices now. A fixed-rate instrument promises fixed amounts. If the market's required return rises, the only thing that can adjust is the price, and it falls. If the required return falls, the price rises. This inverse relationship between rates and the price of a fixed-income instrument is the most important mechanical fact in Section B, and Chapter 4 measures it.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: one bond, three interest rate environments
Step 1. The bond. A five-year bond with a par value of $1,000 and a 6 per cent annual coupon pays $60 a year and $1,000 at the end.
Step 2. Rates equal the coupon. If the market requires 6 per cent, the promised payments are worth exactly what the bond promises to repay and the bond trades at par, $1,000.
Step 3. Rates rise. If the market now requires 7 per cent, nobody will pay $1,000 for a 6 per cent coupon when a new bond pays 7. The price falls until the total return matches: a little under $960 on these terms, and the bond trades at a discount.
Step 4. Rates fall. If the market requires 5 per cent, the 6 per cent coupon is worth having and the price rises above par, to a little over $1,040. The bond trades at a premium.
Check. Nothing about the issuer changed in any of the three cases. The coupon is fixed, the maturity is fixed, and the price did all the moving. The yield to maturity — the rate that makes the promised payments worth the market price — is 6, 7 and 5 per cent in the three cases, and it is the number a buyer actually earns.
Figure F603-03 collects the three.
Figure F603-03. Price, coupon and the market rate
@table
Market required return | Price against par | Called | Coupon rate against yield
Below the coupon rate | Above par | A premium bond | Coupon higher than yield
Equal to the coupon rate | At par | A par bond | Coupon equals yield
Above the coupon rate | Below par | A discount bond | Coupon lower than yield
@end
Inflation reaches shares as well, and less predictably. A company whose prices rise with inflation passes the cost on and its nominal earnings grow; one locked into fixed selling prices does not. Higher nominal rates also raise the discount rate applied to those earnings, which lowers the value of a given stream. Which effect dominates is an empirical question and the exam treats shares as a partial inflation hedge: better than a fixed-rate bond, worse than an asset whose price moves with the price level directly.
