3.3  What a bond is
A bond is a contract to pay stated amounts on stated dates. Everything else about it is a feature, and the exam asks for the features by name.
Par value, also called face value or principal, is the amount repaid at maturity and the base on which the coupon is computed. The convention is $1,000 in the United States.
Maturity is the date the principal is repaid. Short-term paper runs to a year, notes to about ten, bonds beyond that; the terms overlap and the exam uses whatever the question says.
The coupon rate is the annual interest as a percentage of par. A 6 per cent coupon on a $1,000 bond pays $60 a year, usually in two instalments of $30. A zero-coupon bond pays nothing until maturity and is sold at a deep discount, so the whole return is the rise to par. A floating-rate note resets its coupon periodically against a reference rate, which removes most of the price risk and gives the holder rate risk instead.
Redemption provisions say how and when the principal comes back. A call provision lets the issuer repay early, normally at a stated premium above par, and it is valuable to the issuer and costly to the holder: the issuer will call when rates have fallen, which is exactly when the holder would rather keep the bond. A put provision is the mirror image, letting the holder demand early repayment. A sinking fund requires the issuer to retire part of the issue each year, which reduces the risk of a single large repayment and is usually welcomed by holders. Serial bonds mature in instalments by design.
Conversion provisions let the holder exchange the bond for a fixed number of shares. The option is worth something, so a convertible carries a lower coupon than the same company's ordinary debt, and Chapter 7 prices what the issuer gives away.
Covenants are promises in the indenture, which is the contract between the issuer and the trustee acting for the holders. Affirmative covenants require the issuer to do something: maintain insurance, supply audited statements, keep a current ratio above a stated figure. Negative covenants forbid something: further borrowing beyond a stated debt-to-equity ratio, the sale of major assets, dividends beyond a limit. Book 1 met one of these when Barada breached a debt-to-equity covenant at 1.50 and the bank waived it.
Security ranks the claim. A mortgage bond is secured on named property; a debenture is unsecured and relies on the general credit of the issuer; subordinated debt stands behind other debt in a liquidation. The weaker the claim, the higher the coupon.
Figure F603-04 collects the features.
Figure F603-04. The anatomy of a bond
@table
Feature | What it fixes | Who it favours | Effect on the coupon demanded
Par value and maturity | How much comes back, and when | Neutral | Longer maturity, higher rate
Coupon rate | The periodic payment | Neutral | The price adjusts if it is wrong
Call provision | Early repayment at the issuer's choice | The issuer | Higher
Put provision | Early repayment at the holder's choice | The holder | Lower
Sinking fund | Gradual retirement of the issue | The holder | Lower
Conversion | Exchange into shares | The holder | Much lower
Covenants | What the issuer may and may not do | The holder | Lower
Security | Rank in a liquidation | The holder, if secured | Lower if secured
@end
EXAM TRAP   Coupon rate is not yield
The coupon rate is fixed at issue and never changes. The current yield is the coupon over the current price. The yield to maturity is the return earned by a buyer holding to maturity, and it is the one that answers "what does this bond pay now".
A call provision raises the coupon the issuer must offer, because it takes something from the holder. A sinking fund and a conversion right lower it, because they give something.
A bond trading at a discount has a yield above its coupon rate; at a premium, below.
