3.4  Three yields, and the one that matters
A bond quoted at $950 with a 6 per cent coupon and five years to run has three rates attached to it, and the exam will use all three in one question.
The coupon rate is 6 per cent, fixed at issue on the par value. It tells you the cash payment, $60 a year, and nothing about what a buyer earns.
The current yield is the coupon over the market price: 60 ÷ 950 = 6.32 per cent. It is a quick measure of the cash return and it ignores the $50 the holder will gain when $1,000 is repaid.
The yield to maturity is the rate that makes all the promised payments worth exactly $950 today. It is found properly by trial or by a calculator, and the exam accepts a standard approximation: the annual coupon plus the annual amortisation of the discount, over the average of par and price. Here the discount is 1,000 − 950 = 50 spread over five years, which is 10 a year, so the numerator is 60 + 10 = 70; the average of par and price is 975; and the yield is 70 ÷ 975 = 7.18 per cent.
So the same bond pays 6, 6.32 or 7.18 per cent depending on which question is being asked, and only the last is the return a buyer earns by holding to maturity.
Two more yields appear in questions on callable bonds. The yield to call computes the same thing to the call date at the call price, and the prudent figure to quote on a callable bond trading above par is the lower of yield to maturity and yield to call. The realised yield assumes a sale before maturity at an assumed price.
Figure F603-05 sets out the four.
Figure F603-05. Four yields on one bond
@table
Measure | What it divides | On this bond | What it ignores
Coupon rate | Coupon over par | 6.00% | The market price entirely
Current yield | Coupon over price | 6.32% | The gain to par at maturity
Yield to maturity | All payments over price | 7.18% | Reinvestment risk
Yield to call | Payments to the call date | Depends on the call terms | What happens if it is not called
@end
