3.5  Reading the structure as a borrower
Everything above is written from the lender's side. Turn it round, because a candidate works for the issuer.
The maturity choice is the first decision, and it is a trade-off the exam states as a rule. Borrowing short is usually cheaper, because the curve usually slopes upward, and it exposes the company to refinancing at whatever rate prevails when the loan falls due. Borrowing long costs more and fixes the cost. The maturity-matching principle — which Chapter 18 of this book develops for working capital — says to fund long-lived assets with long-dated money and short-lived assets with short-dated money, so that a refinancing never arrives before the asset has paid for itself.
Orontes's own position reads cleanly against that rule. It carries $6,000,000 of long-term debt against $14,802,000 of net property, plant and equipment, with only $400,000 falling due within the year, and it has $7,302,100 of cash. Its plant is funded long, its working capital is funded by suppliers and by its own cash, and its refinancing risk in any one year is small. Book 1 called the maturity profile gentle; this is what gentle means.
The second decision is fixed against floating. Fixed-rate debt makes the cost certain and the value of the liability move with rates. Floating-rate debt keeps the value near par and makes the cost uncertain. A company whose revenue rises with inflation can carry floating-rate debt more comfortably than one whose revenue is contractually fixed, because its receipts and its payments move together.
The third decision is what to give away. Every feature in section 3.3 that favours the holder lowers the coupon, and every feature that favours the issuer raises it. A company that wants the right to refinance cheaply pays for it in the coupon from the first day; a company that accepts tight covenants is paid for them in the same way, and then has to live inside them. Barada, whose covenant at 1.50 was already breached when it wanted to borrow, is the illustration of what living inside them means.
