Chapter summary
Figure F603-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F603-07. Chapter 3 summary map: the key rule for each section.
The term structure is the pattern of yields across maturities, normally upward-sloping, and three theories explain it: expectations, which reads an inverted curve as a forecast of falling rates; liquidity preference, which adds a premium for length and explains why upward is the normal case; and segmentation, which lets supply and demand at each maturity set the rate there. Inside every nominal rate sits expected inflation, multiplicatively: 1.02 × 1.03 = 1.0506, so a 2 per cent real return against 3 per cent inflation needs 5.06 per cent — and because a bond's payments are fixed, a change in the required return moves the price instead, down when rates rise and up when they fall. A bond is a contract described by its par value, maturity and coupon, and modified by call, put, sinking-fund and conversion provisions, by covenants in its indenture and by its security, each of which raises or lowers the coupon according to which side it favours. The same bond has three yields — 6.00 per cent coupon, 60 ÷ 950 = 6.32 per cent current, and 70 ÷ 975 = 7.18 per cent to maturity — and only the last is what a buyer earns. And from the issuer's side the structure is three decisions: how long to borrow, fixed or floating, and what to give away in features, which is why Orontes's $6,000,000 of long-term debt against $14,802,000 of plant reads as a gentle profile and Barada's breached covenant does not.
