Case-style practice: Orontes refinances
Orontes's $6,000,000 of long-term debt carries a fixed 6.5 per cent coupon and matures in four years. The one-year government yield is 3.0 per cent and the ten-year is 5.0 per cent; Orontes borrows at about two points above the government curve. The treasurer proposes repaying the bond early and refinancing with a one-year facility rolled over annually, saving about 1.5 points of interest a year.
Required. Compute the approximate annual saving and the two rates being compared. Say whether you would accept the proposal, naming the risks it creates and one feature of the existing bond you would check first. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC3-1  Answer A. Liquidity preference is a term premium for length.
B is wrong. That is the expectations theory.
C is wrong. A recession signal is an inverted curve, not an upward one.
D is wrong. Policy affects the short end; the slope has its own explanation.
SC3-2  Answer A. Long rates below short rates imply expected falls.
B is wrong. Expected rises produce an upward slope.
C is wrong. Rising inflation expectations lift the long end.
D is wrong. Default expectations affect spreads, not the government curve.
SC3-3  Answer A. Each maturity has its own supply and demand.
B is wrong. The central bank sets the very short end only.
C is wrong. That is closer to the expectations theory.
D is wrong. Segmentation means maturities are not linked in that way.
SC3-4  Answer A. 1.02 × 1.03 = 1.0506.
B is wrong. This subtracts inflation instead of adding it.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. This divides the real rate by inflation.
SC3-5  Answer A. The fixed payments are made in cheaper money.
B is wrong. The lender receives the cheaper money.
C is wrong. The transfer is real in both directions.
D is wrong. A fixed coupon does not change.
SC3-6  Answer A. The payments are fixed, so the price adjusts downwards.
B is wrong. Prices and rates move in opposite directions.
C is wrong. Something must adjust, and it is the price.
D is wrong. The issuer's profit affects the credit spread, not this mechanism.
SC3-7  Answer A. The issuer calls when it can refinance more cheaply.
B is wrong. That describes a put provision.
C is wrong. The option has value to one side.
D is wrong. The trustee acts for the holders and holds no option.
SC3-8  Answer A. A conversion right is valuable to the holder, so the coupon can be lower.
B is wrong. A call provision takes value from the holder, so the coupon is higher.
C is wrong. Subordination weakens the claim and raises the coupon.
D is wrong. A longer maturity usually raises the rate.
SC3-9  Answer A. It requires the issuer to maintain something.
B is wrong. A negative covenant forbids an action.
C is wrong. A call provision concerns repayment.
D is wrong. A sinking fund concerns retirement of the issue.
SC3-10  Answer A. 60 ÷ 950 = 6.32 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the coupon rate on par.
C is wrong. 7.18 per cent is the approximate yield to maturity.
D is wrong. This divides the coupon by par and then by something else.
SC3-11  Answer A. The buyer also gains the rise to par, so the yield exceeds the coupon.
B is wrong. That is the premium case.
C is wrong. They are equal only at par.
D is wrong. The current yield ignores the gain to par.
SC3-12  Answer A. Fund long assets with long money.
B is wrong. That is the aggressive approach and it maximises refinancing risk.
C is wrong. Equity funding of everything is possible and expensive.
D is wrong. Funding stock with thirty-year money is the opposite error.
SC3-13  Answer A. Short money is cheaper on an upward curve and must be refinanced.
B is wrong. Short borrowing is the cheaper of the two.
C is wrong. Fixing the cost requires long-dated money.
D is wrong. Refinancing is precisely interest rate risk.
SC3-14  Answer A. Covenants protect holders, so they accept a lower coupon.
B is wrong. The direction is the opposite.
C is wrong. Every feature prices into the coupon.
D is wrong. Covenants are standard in listed debt.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Shape of the curve | Upward-sloping: the ten-year yield of 5.0 per cent is two points above the one-year 3.0 per cent.
Orontes's ten-year borrowing cost | About 7.0 per cent: 5.0 + 2.0 = 7.0.
Nominal rate wanted | 6.6 per cent: 1.025 × 1.04 = 1.066.
Current yield | 8.00 per cent: 70 ÷ 875 = 8.00 per cent.
Approximate yield to maturity | About 10.8 per cent: the discount is 1,000 − 875 = 125 over four years, which is 31.25 a year, so the numerator is 70 + 31.25 = 101.25 and the average of par and price is 937.50, giving 101.25 ÷ 937.50 = 10.80 per cent.
What it tells you | The bond's 7 per cent coupon says almost nothing about what a buyer earns. At $875 the buyer earns about 10.8 per cent, which is three points above the coupon and a point above what the curve plus a credit spread would suggest — so either the market thinks this issuer is riskier than a two-point spread, or the bond is cheap.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P3-01  Answer A. Upward-sloping is the normal case.
B is wrong. Inversions are occasional.
C is wrong. Flat curves occur at turning points.
D is wrong. A U-shape is not a standard description.
P3-02  Answer A. Long rates above short rates imply expected rises.
B is wrong. Expected falls invert the curve.
C is wrong. A flat curve implies holding.
D is wrong. Nothing in the slope implies negative rates.
P3-03  Answer A. Lenders require a premium for length.
B is wrong. Inflation forecasts belong to the expectations theory.
C is wrong. Issuance belongs to segmentation.
D is wrong. Segmentation is a separate theory.
P3-04  Answer A. 1.025 × 1.04 = 1.066.
B is wrong. That is the addition approximation, which is close and not exact.
C is wrong. This subtracts inflation.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
P3-05  Answer A. A coupon above the market rate is worth paying for.
B is wrong. Discounts arise when rates rise above the coupon.
C is wrong. Par requires the two to be equal.
D is wrong. The call price applies only if the bond is called.
P3-06  Answer A. The call is the issuer's option.
B is wrong. A put is the holder's option.
C is wrong. Conversion exchanges the bond for shares.
D is wrong. A sinking fund retires the issue gradually by agreement.
P3-07  Answer A. Negative covenants forbid.
B is wrong. That is an affirmative covenant.
C is wrong. The coupon is set at issue.
D is wrong. Government bonds rarely carry covenants of this kind.
P3-08  Answer A. 70 ÷ 875 = 8.00 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the coupon on par.
C is wrong. This divides by a figure above par.
D is wrong. This divides par by the price.
P3-09  Answer A. The buyer pays above par and receives par, so the yield is below the coupon.
B is wrong. That is the discount case.
C is wrong. They differ unless the bond is at par.
D is wrong. It is computable in the ordinary way.
P3-10  Answer A. Match the life of the asset to the life of the money.
B is wrong. Equity funding is not matching.
C is wrong. Overdraft funding of long assets is the aggressive extreme.
D is wrong. That reverses the principle.
Case-style set: Orontes refinances
The two rates. The existing bond costs 6.5 per cent fixed for four more years. One-year money at two points above a 3.0 per cent government yield costs 3.0 + 2.0 = 5.0 per cent today. The saving is 1.5 points on $6,000,000, which is 6,000,000 × 0.015 = 90,000 a year before any costs of the change.
The recommendation. I would not accept it as proposed. The saving is real and it is the price of three risks the proposal does not mention.
The first is refinancing risk. The company would be committing to borrow again in twelve months at whatever rate exists then. The curve itself says the market expects short rates to rise — two points between one year and ten — so on the market's own expectations the saving shrinks and may reverse within the four years.
The second is renewal risk, which is worse than rate risk. A one-year facility has to be granted again, and a bank's willingness depends on the company's condition at that date. Book 1 found a gross margin falling a point a year; a lender reading that in two years' time may renew on tighter terms, or at a smaller amount, or not at all.
The third is maturity mismatch. The debt funds plant with a long life, and the proposal would fund it with money that has to be found again every year.
The feature I would check first is the call provision. If the bond is not callable, the proposal cannot be executed at all without negotiating a redemption with the holders, which normally costs a premium — and that premium, spread over four years, can consume the whole $90,000 saving.
